<!-- ═══════════════════════════════ SECTION 6: KEY FINDINGS ══════════════ -->
<section class="report-section" id="key-findings">
  <div class="section-header">
    <span class="section-num">Section 06</span>
    <h2 class="section-title">Key Findings &amp; Policy Implications</h2>
  </div>

  <p style="margin-bottom:20px">The following findings are derived exclusively from the 50,000-record sample analysed. Each finding is linked to a concrete policy implication for the fuel market regulation and consumer protection agenda.</p>

  <ul class="findings-list">

    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">1</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="finding-title">Diesel price dispersion is significantly higher than petrol — and materially expensive for consumers</div>
        <div class="finding-text">Standard diesel (B7_STANDARD) shows an IQR of <strong>33.0p/l</strong> (19.7% of median), more than double that of E10 petrol (IQR: 16.0p/l, 11.0%). A driver using a 50-litre tank who fills at a P75 vs. P25 forecourt pays <strong>£16.50 more per fill</strong> — over £850/year for weekly fillers. Premium diesel (B7_PREMIUM) shows a similarly wide IQR of 30.0p/l.</div>
        <div class="finding-policy"><b>Policy implication:</b> The diesel market shows weaker price convergence than petrol. Regulators should consider whether the open data scheme is generating sufficient competitive pressure in diesel pricing, and whether targeted consumer communications (e.g. best-price alerts) are warranted for B7_STANDARD users.</div>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">2</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="finding-title">The government price feed is predominantly real-time, but a stale tail merits attention</div>
        <div class="finding-text">80% of records carry a lag of under 1 hour between retailer update and data capture, with a median lag of just <strong>3 minutes</strong>. However, <strong>3.7% of records (≈1,802) are over 7 days stale</strong>, and the worst case is 52+ days old. These stale records may be actively displayed to consumers via the Fuel Finder service, undermining the scheme's transparency goal.</div>
        <div class="finding-policy"><b>Policy implication:</b> Introduce a mandatory staleness threshold (e.g. 48 hours) after which records are flagged or suppressed in consumer-facing interfaces. Stations with persistently stale data should be identified for compliance review.</div>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">3</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="finding-title">HVO (renewable diesel) has the poorest reporting discipline of mainstream fuel types</div>
        <div class="finding-text">HVO records show a median lag of <strong>1.04 hours</strong> (vs. ~3 minutes for mainstream fuels) and only 48.3% are fresh within 1 hour. While the HVO sample is small (91 records, 54 stations), it represents an emerging fuel category relevant to net-zero targets. Poor price visibility may deter consumer uptake.</div>
        <div class="finding-policy"><b>Policy implication:</b> As the government promotes alternative fuels, HVO stations should receive specific guidance on feed update obligations. Incentives or audits targeting the green fuel segment would improve data reliability in this growth area.</div>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">4</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="finding-title">Premium fuel premiums are substantial and warrant scrutiny</div>
        <div class="finding-text">B7_PREMIUM diesel commands a median premium of <strong>20.0p/l</strong> over B7_STANDARD (187.9p vs. 167.9p). E5 super unleaded costs a median of <strong>16.0p/l</strong> more than E10 (160.9p vs. 144.9p). These spreads are significant in absolute consumer cost terms; the B7_PREMIUM premium alone adds ~£10 per 50-litre tank fill.</div>
        <div class="finding-policy"><b>Policy implication:</b> Regulators should monitor whether premium fuel mark-ups reflect genuine product differentiation costs or reflect reduced competitive pressure at the premium end of the market. The wide IQR of B7_PREMIUM (30p/l) suggests limited price discovery for premium diesel buyers.</div>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">5</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="finding-title">10.8% of stations report only a single fuel type — potential compliance gap</div>
        <div class="finding-text">796 forecourts (10.8%) report just one fuel type in this dataset. While some may be genuine specialty operators (e.g. HVO-only or fleet diesel), others may be partially non-compliant with the UK mandatory open data reporting requirement, which mandates reporting of all fuel types sold.</div>
        <div class="finding-policy"><b>Policy implication:</b> A targeted audit of single-fuel-type reporting stations could identify whether this represents operational specialisation or selective compliance. Cross-referencing with operator licensing data (outside the scope of this dataset) would quantify the non-compliance risk.</div>
      </div>
    </li>

    <li>
      <div class="finding-num">6</div>
      <div class="finding-body">
        <div class="finding-title">Data integrity anomalies (negative lags, extreme outliers) suggest feed quality controls are insufficient</div>
        <div class="finding-text">2.0% of records (1,011) carry a source timestamp <em>after</em> the recorded timestamp — physically impossible in a correct feed. A further 174 records (0.35%) carry prices outside any plausible range (0p to 1,889p/l). These anomalies are distributed across fuel types and are unlikely to be isolated incidents.</div>
        <div class="finding-policy"><b>Policy implication:</b> The scheme administrator should implement automated validation rules at feed ingestion: reject prices outside a plausible range (e.g. 80–300p), flag timestamps where source &gt; recorded, and provide quarterly data quality reports to participating retailers. A cleaner feed directly improves consumer trust in the Fuel Finder service.</div>
      </div>
    </li>

  </ul>
</section>

